// Order Blocks helper library — extracted from CBC v4.85 (wCBC) to move these box-array utilities
// out of the main script's compiled-token budget, since Pine's CE10117 ceiling applies only to the
// main script; a published library compiles separately and doesn't count against it. These three
// functions are pure box-array utilities with no dependency on any of CBC's own internal state — all
// context (colors, counts, coordinates) is passed in as parameters — which is what makes them safe to
// pull out without touching CBC's own logic at all.
//
// v3 (09/09/2026): added 6 small pure-parameter formatting/string helpers moved out of the main CBC
// script for the same reason (CE10117 budget relief) — f_order3, f_price_vs_emas, f_struct_abbrev,
// f_prox_crossing, f_prox_approaching, f_fmt_ny_time. None of these touch CBC's own internal state;
// f_prox_crossing/f_prox_approaching now take their distance thresholds as explicit parameters
// (previously read the main script's own input.float globals directly, which a library can't do),
// and f_fmt_ny_time hardcodes "America/New_York" internally since that was always a fixed constant,
// never a user input.
//
// v4 (09/09/2026): added 4 more pure helpers for the same reason — f_status_pos (table-position
// string -> position.*), f_table_text_size (size-label string -> size.*), f_chart_tf_disp (chart
// timeframe -> display string, e.g. "15m"), f_htf_tf_disp (a timeframe string -> the same display
// format). All 4 only ever read their own parameters plus Pine's built-in timeframe.*/size.*/
// position.* namespaces — none of them touch any of CBC's own state. f_chart_tf_disp/f_htf_tf_disp
// are renamed from the main script's chart_tf_disp()/htf_tf_disp() purely for naming consistency
// with the rest of this library's f_-prefixed exports — no functional difference.
//
// v5 (09/10/2026): added 6 more pure-parameter helpers for the same reason (CE10117 relief round 3,
// main script over by 771 at v6.39) — f_prox_latch_name, f_htf_anchor_long, f_htf_anchor_short,
// f_level_label_text, f_apn, f_aplus_pivot_dist. Three of these (f_level_label_text, f_apn,
// f_aplus_pivot_dist) originally read the main script's own input.* globals directly (show_level_name/
// show_level_price, aplus_pivot_dist, aplus_pivot_dist_override) — a library can't do that, so each
// gained an explicit parameter for whatever it used to read from outer scope, same adaptation already
// used for f_prox_crossing/f_prox_approaching in v3. f_htf_anchor_long/f_htf_anchor_short read only
// bar_index/time[]/low[]/high[] — Pine's own built-in series, available from library scope the same
// way f_obAdd already reads high[_idx]/low[_idx]/bar_index — so no parameter changes needed for those
// two beyond their existing signature.
//
// v6 (09/13/2026): added f_paLabel — a pure box/label-style helper (coords/text/color/size all passed
// in as parameters, no CBC-internal state) for the new Price Action Structure (Internal/Swing HH/HL/
// LH/LL) markers. Same proven-safe category as f_obAdd/f_htfObAdd: creates one label per call, no
// array management needed (unlike Order Blocks' boxes) since these markers are meant to persist as a
// historical record on the chart rather than being deleted/managed.
//
// v13 (09/15/2026): added f_orbRetestMarker — a pure box+label helper for the 6 near-identical ORB
// Retest marker call sites (Pass/Flip/Fail-from, both directions) in the main script, each of which
// was a hand-written box.new()+label.new() pair differing only in y-offset/half-height/border color/
// tooltip text — all now passed in as parameters. Same proven-safe "pass state in as parameters,
// create inside, no CBC-internal state read" category as f_obAdd/f_paLabel/f_levelLabel above. Added
// as part of a CE10117 token-budget trim (100606/100256) alongside a main-script loop consolidation
// for the Session ORB Cross feature's 3 named-session branches.
//
// v15 (09/17/2026): added f_fmt_ny_time_sec — a seconds-resolution sibling of f_fmt_ny_time, per
// direct request ("can the time in the tooltip include seconds"). Scoped to tooltips ONLY, confirmed
// via follow-up: the Status table, Historical Bar Panel, and alert emails all keep using the existing
// minute-only f_fmt_ny_time unchanged, so their display format doesn't shift for anyone. This new
// function is a straight copy of f_fmt_ny_time's body with one added ":SS" segment (zero-padded same
// way H/MM already are) — same "America/New_York" hardcoded internally, same na-guard returning "—".
// Main script call sites updated: the LTF CBC Reversal triangle tooltip, the confirmed HTF CBC Flip
// dot's "Confirmed: " tooltip line (shared with the HTF Reversal gold-dot tooltip), and the
// anticipatory HTF CBC Flip dot's "Fired: " tooltip line — all 3 are pure tooltip-only call sites (no
// Status table cell, Historical Bar Panel row, or alert email reuses them), confirmed via grep before
// changing anything.
//
// v16 (09/17/2026): CE10117 relief (100457/100256, over by 201) — the v7.08 LTF CBC Reversal tooltip
// was the newest, priciest piece of inline string-building in the main script (a brand-new multi-part
// tooltip= argument -- ticker + label text + a ternary + a timestamp call -- where none existed
// before v7.08). Moved that whole tooltip-string assembly into this library as f_ltf_reversal_tooltip,
// same proven-safe "pass state in as parameters, build/return string, no CBC-internal state read"
// category as f_vwap_flip_warning/f_pivot_flip_warning above (ticker/is_bull/t are all passed in; it
// calls this library's own f_fmt_ny_time_sec internally, no cross-library dependency). Main script
// call site collapses from a full inline concatenation to a single function call.
//
// v17 (09/17/2026): CE10117 relief round 2, same report (100333/100256, over by 77 after v16's fix
// brought it down from 201). Two more small moves, both flagged as "next targets" in the main script's
// own v7.10 changelog: (1) f_htf_is_reversal — the small boolean expression behind the v7.08
// htf_reversal named var (bull/bear OR, a bar-count subtraction, an equality check) moved here as a
// pure 4-parameter function; the main script still keeps its own local htf_reversal var (it's read at
// 2 sites there) but that var's right-hand side is now one function call instead of the full inline
// expression. (2) f_htf_reversal_note — the static "⚠ HTF Reversal..." tooltip string itself, with no
// parameters at all, so the main script calls it instead of carrying the string literal. Same
// proven-safe "pure parameters/no parameters in, CBC-internal state never read" category as
// f_ltf_reversal_tooltip above.
//
// v18 (09/18/2026, SUPERSEDED by v19 below): first CE10117 relief attempt for v7.14 (main script
// reported 100551/100256, over by 295) — added an array-based f_resolve_flip_early. This made the
// main script's count WORSE (100582, up from 100551): wrapping every read/write of the 6 dot/arrow
// tracking vars in array.get()/array.set() at ~9 touch points cost more than the resolver logic it
// replaced ever did. Left in the file history here as a record of what NOT to do — v19 replaced this
// signature entirely with a scalar version plus 2 new draw-site exports, see below.
//
// v19 (09/18/2026): CE10117 relief round 2 for v7.14/v7.15 (v7.14 reported 100582/100256, over by 326
// — worse than v7.13's original 100551). Reworked f_resolve_flip_early back to 8 plain scalar
// parameters (label/pred/wait for the dot, same 3 for the arrow, plus the confirmed direction and the
// live HTF bar identity) instead of arrays — a single call now resolves both the dot's and the arrow's
// pending prediction and returns a 2-value tuple (each one's new wait-time) for the main script to
// reassign onto its own persistent vars. More importantly, added 2 new exports that move the actual
// DRAW-SITE label.new() calls into the library, not just the resolver: f_flip_early_dot (builds the
// anticipatory dot's full tooltip — ticker + direction + seconds-before-close + a timestamp call +
// EMA-distance string + an optional detailed-snapshot line — and creates the label) and
// f_flip_early_arrow (builds the anticipatory arrow's label, with its own y-offset/color/style
// ternaries). This is the same proven-safe "pass state in as parameters, no CBC-internal state read"
// category as f_orbRetestMarker/f_paLabel/f_levelLabel above — the main script's call sites now just
// assign the returned label directly, same ":= label.new(...)" idiom pointed at a function call.
//
// v20 (09/19/2026): CE10117 relief for v7.18 (main script reported 104160/100256, over by 3904).
// Added f_next_two_levels — the entire "next two levels" scan for the CBC main script's Next Target
// Levels / Next Stop Levels Status-table rows (items 3/4 of its Pre-Trade Checklist), moved here as
// a pure 4-parameter function: a price array, a matching label array, the live trade-direction bool,
// and the current close. No CBC-internal state read beyond what's passed in — generalized to any
// pool size via array.size() rather than a hardcoded count, so the main script can grow its level
// pool later without needing this function touched again. Same proven-safe "pass state in as
// parameters, build/return values" category as f_vwap_flip_warning/f_pivot_flip_warning above.
//
